{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1788304763,"nonce":"Z2hytCMvtWEfRNMAYAjpkg0qoNo=","title":"What else should wake the agent? A day of quiet is only safe to sleep through if the halt set is good enough","message":"Filed from `1113019` on Evan's own instruction — its ruling (comment #3) says the question \"is worth its own issue rather than smuggled in here\", and the fix that shipped there makes it live.\n\n## Why this exists now\n\n`1113019` set the default bound on an `until` advance to 60,000 ticks — one in-game day — and Evan's reasoning reframed what the number is for:\n\n\u003e \"a full day without doing anything while you're fully set is pretty typical. Lots of things the colony does itself day to day and ideally if something bad happens, you'll be woken up, you won't have to check.\"\n\nThat is a design statement about the whole loop, and it has a consequence the bound itself does not discharge: **the value of the bound is \"eventually hand control back\", not \"catch a bug\", which means the HALT SET is carrying the safety.** The agent is supposed to be able to say \"advance\" and go away, and the halts are the wake-up mechanism.\n\n**A day of quiet is only safe to sleep through if the halt set is good enough, and right now it is one halt.** `722c951`'s own-faction casualty halt is doing the whole job alone. That halt was designed as a guard rail; `1113019` promoted it to the primary interrupt without anyone asking whether it is sufficient.\n\n## What halts exist today\n\nFrom `TimeDriver`'s reason list:\n\n| reason | fires on | opt-in? |\n|---|---|---|\n| `casualty` | an OWN-FACTION pawn goes down or dies | default-on (`722c951`) |\n| `dialog` | a force-pausing modal is up | default-on (1.7) |\n| `red_error` | a red error | default-on (`halt_on_error`) |\n| `stalled` | the clock stopped for a reason that is not ours | default-on (1.8) |\n| `letter` / `threat` / `alert` / `event` | the caller ASKED for it | opt-in matcher |\n| `condition` / `layout` | the caller ASKED for it | opt-in matcher |\n| `timeout` / `ticks` / `interrupted` | the caller's own terms | — |\n\nSo the only default-on halts that are about **the colony** rather than about the **mod or the UI** are `casualty` and `red_error`. Everything else that a colony can do to itself in a day is reachable only by a caller that already knew to ask.\n\n## The gap, concretely\n\nAn in-game day of quiet can contain, with no halt at all:\n\n* **A raid letter** (`ThreatBig`). `until:{threat}` exists and is opt-in; an advance armed on a `condition` gets no threat halt whatever. An agent asleep on a `condition` predicate sleeps through a raid.\n* **A breakdown / power loss.** A generator fails, the freezer warms, the food spoils. Nothing is emitted that halts.\n* **A mood collapse into a break.** `Alert_...` alerts go active; `until:{alert}` is opt-in.\n* **A food cliff.** `resources.food_days` crossing a floor is exactly the `condition` shape, and `Alert_LowFood` is a lagging indicator (DESIGN says so).\n* **A colonist starving, or a fire.** Same class.\n\nNone of these are new capabilities — the journal already emits letters and alert transitions, `AlertScanner` already polls, `DigestVerb` already publishes `resources`/`power`/`threats`. **The question is which of them should halt an advance BY DEFAULT**, in the way `casualty` does, rather than only when the caller thought to ask.\n\n## Why \"just arm a matcher\" is not the answer\n\nTwo structural reasons, both already in the repo:\n\n1. **One matcher per advance.** `TimeDriver.CheckUntilKeys` refuses a second — deliberately (`fc287ba`), because a silently-outranked matcher is `7382bdd`'s class with ten in-game days attached. So an agent that wants \"advance until the room is built, but wake me on a raid\" cannot spell it today. That is the whole shape of the problem.\n2. **A default-on halt and an opt-in matcher are different things.** `casualty` is not a matcher and is not opt-in precisely because \"the pawn you were told about died during the next advance\" was the M1 failure verbatim. The same argument applies to at least some of the list above, and the issue is deciding WHICH — a halt set that is too eager makes every fight a wedge (`722c951`'s own reasoning for not halting on a hostile downing).\n\n## What this issue must decide\n\n* **The default-on halt set.** Which colony events wake the agent unasked. Argue each from a real failure, not from a category — the project's rule is candidates and reasons, never bare booleans, and a halt nobody can justify from a transcript is a wedge waiting to happen.\n* **The escape, and its shape.** `722c951` established that a bypass must be per-call, carry a required non-empty reason string, and be journaled as an act. A new default-on halt needs the same, and it should probably be ONE control rather than one per halt — `advance {through_casualties:\"…\"}` is already the pattern.\n* **Whether the \"one matcher\" rule needs to become \"one matcher plus a standing wake set\".** It probably does, and that is the design decision, not an implementation detail.\n* **What it costs.** `fc287ba`'s predicate-cost decision is the precedent: a halt that is expensive per frame is a defect even if correct, and the measurement is the control. The banked numbers say polling is currently free (`evaluations: 419` over `frames: 6287` at `eval_ms_avg: 0.0172`, total 7.19 ms across three in-game days), so there is headroom — but it has to be measured, not assumed.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- The default-on halt set is DECIDED and recorded in DESIGN's decisions log, with each member justified from a real observed failure (a transcript, a bench run, or an M1 finding) rather than from a category.\n- Each new default-on halt reports a `reason` token and a `halted_on` block naming what fired, on the same terms `casualty` already does.\n- Every new default-on halt is bypassable per-call, with a required non-empty reason string, journaled as an `action` row and echoed on the envelope — `722c951`'s three controls, unchanged.\n- An advance armed on `until:{condition}` or `until:{layout}` still wakes on the standing set. Proven, because this is the case the \"one matcher\" rule currently forbids and it is the case the whole issue is about.\n- A hostile downing, a hostile death and an ordinary neutral letter do NOT halt. Proven, because a halt set that stops on every fight is a wedge and `722c951` already ruled on that shape.\n- The per-frame cost of the standing set is MEASURED and published on the advance envelope, the way `until.eval_ms_per_frame` already is. A number, not a promise in a comment.\n- An in-game day of quiet on a healthy colony still returns `reason:\"timeout\"` at 60,000 ticks with no spurious halt. Proven on a bench, because \"a day of quiet is genuinely safe to sleep through\" is the claim `1113019` left standing and this issue has to make true.","files":null}]}